endency on  russian fossil fuels you know that we  have stopped the import of coal that we  are winding down  uh the import of russian oil  it's going to be tough  but we have created regional  groups to make sure that the gas flows  where it is needed  we have emergency plans  if you look at in the wider perspective  i'm deeply convinced that we will  prevail because this is not only  a war that russia has unleashed against  ukraine but this is a question whether  autocracies will prevail or democracy  will prevail and i'm deeply convinced  that we have the longer breath and on  the other hand  if you look our sanctions are more and  more biting and grinding into the  russian economy there's a very  interesting yale study i can only  recommend to look at it  that shows how deep the sanctions are  cutting and this is increasing week by  week that's the way we designed them  yes i know that for quite a long time  putin has been able to partially cushion  this  decrease  or the yeah of the  russian economy by revenues from fossil  fuels  and that's why it's so important that we  keep on track as i said the numbers are  telling that i just displayed yeah most  important of course is that most  important of course is that europe  remains solidare solidaria to one  another  do you trust that all countries will be  solidary yes i am convinced that this is  the case of course we always have to  discuss the details but  we have made a crucial experience  uh during the pandemic  at the very beginning there the member  states tried to go on their own  nationally  closing down borders export bans you  name it and then we realized this is too  big for each one of us  and then we stood together  and we  discovered the strength of sharing  and of solidarity  and as i said much much more than 27 we  are much stronger than 27 countries  together this is the european union  with all the power that is behind it and  if you look in hindsight this region  has gone pretty well through t